    s__CATARACT_WHAT(py_str("A cataract is the lens inside the eye going cloudy, so sight fades slowly and lights glare and smear at night - it is not a film on the surface, and no drops or glasses fix it. It is mostly age, and the operation is one of the commonest and safest there is: the cloudy lens is swapped for a clear plastic one under local anaesthetic, about 20 minutes, and most people see better within days. Ask the optician at your next test if the glare is affecting driving, and tell them if sight drops suddenly, which is not a cataract."));
    s__CATARACT_LENGTH(py_str("About 20 minutes for the operation itself, under local anaesthetic, and most people are in and out of the hospital the same morning or afternoon: the cloudy lens is swapped for a clear plastic one, and sight is usually better within days, though the eye can feel gritty and watery for a week or two. Ask the optician at your next test if the glare is affecting driving, and tell them if sight drops suddenly, which is not a cataract."));
